### Complete Wireshark Display Filters and Their Functions (Ubuntu)

Wireshark uses display filters to help you find, examine and analyse specific network packets. They allow you to isolate traffic from browsers, terminals, servers, protocols and applications.

There are hundreds of Wireshark display filters. Below is a practical reference covering the major filters you should learn for cybersecurity and network analysis.

### 1\. Basic network filters

| Filter | Function                           |
| ------ | ---------------------------------- |
| ip     | Displays IPv4 packets              |
| ipv6   | Displays IPv6 packets              |
| eth    | Displays Ethernet packets          |
| arp    | Displays ARP traffic               |
| icmp   | Displays ICMP packets              |
| icmpv6 | Displays IPv6 ICMP packets         |
| tcp    | Displays TCP traffic               |
| udp    | Displays UDP traffic               |
| dns    | Displays DNS traffic               |
| tls    | Displays TLS encrypted connections |
| http   | Displays HTTP traffic              |
| ftp    | Displays FTP traffic               |
| ssh    | Displays SSH traffic               |
| dhcp   | Displays DHCP traffic              |

### 2\. IP address filters

| Filter                  | Function                          |
| ----------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| ip.addr == 192.168.1.10 | Traffic to or from an IP          |
| ip.src == 192.168.1.10  | Packets coming from an IP         |
| ip.dst == 192.168.1.10  | Packets going to an IP            |
| ip.addr != 192.168.1.10 | Excludes an IP                    |
| ip.src == 192.168.1.1   | Shows packets from a router       |
| ip.dst == 8.8.8.8       | Shows packets going to Google DNS |
| ip.ttl < 64             | Finds packets with TTL below 64   |

Replace the example addresses with the addresses you want to investigate.

